# Bounty tracker -- every 3 hours.
#
# The cadence is set by the inventory, not by competition: ~520 standing
# unclaimed bounties that turn over slowly (measured 2026-08-05). Eight runs a
# day sits far inside both documented quotas.
#
# Wall-clock per run is NOT small, and that is deliberate. Search pages are
# paced and a burst throttle is waited out with doubling backoff, because
# abandoning a throttled query costs most of the inventory. A run that takes
# fifteen minutes and returns everything beats one that takes twenty seconds
# and returns page one.
#
# PATH is explicit: cron gets a minimal environment, and node/claude live under
# the nvm prefix (HANDOFF.md trap #8). hunt.py itself needs only system python3
# and requests.
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/home/ubuntu/.local/bin
0 */3 * * * cd $HOME/expensify-sniper && /usr/bin/python3 hunt.py >> $HOME/expensify-sniper/hunt.log 2>&1

# Payer watch -- every 10 minutes.
#
# A different job from the digest above, because it answers a different
# question. Measured 2026-08-09: proven payers rewarded 501 issues in ninety
# days while holding THREE open unassigned bounties between the ten most recent
# of them. Real bounties are claimed within hours, so a 3-hourly search only
# ever finds what nobody wanted. This catches the label as it lands.
#
# Affordable because it revalidates: /issues/events is core quota (5000/hr) and
# a 304 leaves the quota untouched, so a quiet repo costs nothing. Ten minutes
# is not a preference -- one events page holds 100 entries and covered a single
# DAY on go-gitea/gitea, so a slower sweep loses labels silently.
*/10 * * * * cd $HOME/expensify-sniper && /usr/bin/python3 hunt.py --watch >> $HOME/expensify-sniper/watch.log 2>&1
